<p>What the letter displays is the reading of public pages. A title, a pen name, an Amazon link, and a recent article can be taken from a catalog that is already outside. A sentence of that kind does not prove that anyone read the books, and it does not prove a look at sales.</p>
<p>The commercial shape is ordinary. First it shows knowledge of the works. Then it praises the base that is already there. Then it implies a gap: the two catalogs are, it says, hard to find. A generic remedy follows: a launch calendar, sharper blurbs, and promotion that can be repeated. The question at the end is easy, and it also asks about priority: which book should come first. It does not ask for money yet. It asks that a conversation begin.</p>
